<Positioned left="0" top="0"><Container width="1880" height="6" color="#7C6BF0FF"/></Positioned>
<Positioned left="48" top="34"><Container width="4" height="64.6" color="#7C6BF0FF"/></Positioned>
<Positioned left="66" top="32"><Text fontSize="12" color="#7C6BF0FF" fontFamily="Noto Sans Mono CJK SC" fontStyle="BOLD" letterSpacing="1">STRATOSPHERE REVIEW · ISSUE 07 · 2026</Text></Positioned>
<Positioned left="66" top="52"><Text fontSize="34" color="#F5F8FCFF" fontFamily="Inter" fontStyle="BOLD">Four stages</Text></Positioned>
<Positioned left="66" top="91"><Text fontSize="15" color="#9FB0CCFF" fontFamily="Noto Sans CJK SC" fontStyle="NORMAL">Why the hole opens over one pole, in one season</Text></Positioned>
<Positioned left="66" top="116.94"><Text fontSize="12" color="#9FB0CCFF" fontFamily="Noto Sans Mono CJK SC" fontStyle="NORMAL">The chemistry needs a cold, isolated, dark stratosphere and then sunlight. Antarctica supplies all three in the right order every year — the Arctic only sometimes.</Text></Positioned>
<Positioned left="48" top="146.94"><Text fontSize="13" color="#38BDF8FF" fontFamily="Noto Sans Mono CJK SC" fontStyle="BOLD">FIG. 05</Text></Positioned>
<Positioned left="110" top="146.94"><Text fontSize="12" color="#8FA0BCFF" fontFamily="Noto Sans Mono CJK SC" fontStyle="NORMAL">SCHEMATIC OF THE PUBLISHED MECHANISM · NASA OZONE WATCH</Text></Positioned>
<Positioned left="48" top="206.94"><Container width="434" height="560" color="#16264AFF" borderRadius="10" border="1 SOLID #25375FFF"/></Positioned>
<Positioned left="48" top="206.94"><Container width="434" height="4" color="#38BDF8FF"/></Positioned>
<Positioned left="68" top="224.94"><Text fontSize="26" color="#38BDF8FF" fontFamily="Noto Sans Mono CJK SC" fontStyle="BOLD">01</Text></Positioned>
<Positioned left="110" top="228.94"><Text fontSize="12" color="#8FA0BCFF" fontFamily="Noto Sans Mono CJK SC" fontStyle="BOLD" letterSpacing="1">May–August</Text></Positioned>
<Positioned left="68" top="260.94"><Text fontSize="20" color="#F5F8FCFF" fontFamily="Inter" fontStyle="BOLD">Polar night</Text></Positioned>
<Positioned left="68" top="286.94"><Text fontSize="13" color="#38BDF8FF" fontFamily="Noto Sans Mono CJK SC" fontStyle="BOLD">The vortex isolates the air</Text></Positioned>
<Positioned left="68" top="313"><Text fontSize="13" color="#B9C7DE" fontFamily="Noto Sans CJK SC" fontStyle="NORMAL">An endlessly circling whirlpool of stratospheric</Text></Positioned>
<Positioned left="68" top="331"><Text fontSize="13" color="#B9C7DE" fontFamily="Noto Sans CJK SC" fontStyle="NORMAL">winds cuts the polar air off from the</Text></Positioned>
<Positioned left="68" top="349"><Text fontSize="13" color="#B9C7DE" fontFamily="Noto Sans CJK SC" fontStyle="NORMAL">mid-latitudes. With no sunlight it gets cold</Text></Positioned>
<Positioned left="68" top="367"><Text fontSize="13" color="#B9C7DE" fontFamily="Noto Sans CJK SC" fontStyle="NORMAL">enough for clouds to form even in air this thin</Text></Positioned>
<Positioned left="68" top="385"><Text fontSize="13" color="#B9C7DE" fontFamily="Noto Sans CJK SC" fontStyle="NORMAL">and dry.</Text></Positioned>
