<Positioned left="1521.0" top="528.94" width="140"><Container alignment="CENTER"><Text fontSize="15" color="#F5F8FCFF" fontFamily="Noto Sans Mono CJK SC" fontStyle="BOLD">mixing</Text></Container></Positioned>
<Positioned left="1521.0" top="548.94" width="140"><Container alignment="CENTER"><Text fontSize="12" color="#34D399FF" fontFamily="Noto Sans Mono CJK SC" fontStyle="BOLD">O₃ returns</Text></Container></Positioned>
<Positioned left="1394" top="722.94"><Container width="120.0" height="20" color="#34D39922" borderRadius="10" border="1 SOLID #34D39999"/></Positioned>
<Positioned left="1394" top="725.94" width="120.0"><Container alignment="CENTER"><Text fontSize="10" color="#34D399FF" fontFamily="Noto Sans Mono CJK SC" fontStyle="BOLD">warm air mixes in</Text></Container></Positioned>
<Positioned left="1532" top="722.94"><Container width="84.0" height="20" color="#34D39922" borderRadius="10" border="1 SOLID #34D39999"/></Positioned>
<Positioned left="1532" top="725.94" width="84.0"><Container alignment="CENTER"><Text fontSize="10" color="#34D399FF" fontFamily="Noto Sans Mono CJK SC" fontStyle="BOLD">hole closes</Text></Container></Positioned>
<Positioned left="1670" top="722.94"><Container width="84.0" height="20" color="#34D39922" borderRadius="10" border="1 SOLID #34D39999"/></Positioned>
<Positioned left="1670" top="725.94" width="84.0"><Container alignment="CENTER"><Text fontSize="10" color="#34D399FF" fontFamily="Noto Sans Mono CJK SC" fontStyle="BOLD">O₃ recovers</Text></Container></Positioned>
<Positioned left="48" top="792.94"><Container width="1784" height="118" color="#122040FF" borderRadius="8" border="1 SOLID #25375FFF"/></Positioned>
<Positioned left="72" top="808.94"><Text fontSize="12" color="#F5A524FF" fontFamily="Noto Sans Mono CJK SC" fontStyle="BOLD" letterSpacing="1">WHY THIS ONLY HAPPENS OVER ANTARCTICA</Text></Positioned>
<Positioned left="72" top="833"><Text fontSize="14" color="#B9C7DE" fontFamily="Noto Sans CJK SC" fontStyle="NORMAL">The Arctic has a polar vortex too, but it is warmer and less stable, so polar stratospheric clouds form less reliably and severe ozone loss needs an exceptionally cold winter. Antarctica's</Text></Positioned>
<Positioned left="72" top="852"><Text fontSize="14" color="#B9C7DE" fontFamily="Noto Sans CJK SC" fontStyle="NORMAL">land-locked, high, cold interior makes the vortex reliable every year — which is why the record in this issue is the Antarctic record.</Text></Positioned>
<Positioned left="48" top="1006"><Container width="1784" height="1" color="#7C8CB040"/></Positioned>
<Positioned left="48" top="1018" width="1784"><Container alignment="CENTER_LEFT"><Text fontSize="11" color="#6C7C99FF" fontFamily="Noto Sans Mono CJK SC" fontStyle="NORMAL">SOURCES  S2 NASA Ozone Watch — What is the Ozone Hole? · S3 NASA Ozone Watch — What is Ozone?</Text></Container></Positioned>
<Positioned left="48" top="1036" width="1784"><Container alignment="CENTER_LEFT"><Text fontSize="11" color="#6C7C99FF" fontFamily="Noto Sans Mono CJK SC" fontStyle="NORMAL">These four panels are labelled schematics of the mechanism described in the sources. They contain no measured values: temperatures, altitudes and particle counts are illustrative of the published description, not data.</Text></Container></Positioned>
<Positioned left="48" top="1058"><Text fontSize="11" color="#6C7C99FF" fontFamily="Noto Sans Mono CJK SC" fontStyle="NORMAL">STRATOSPHERE REVIEW · ISSUE 07 · FIG. 05</Text></Positioned>
<Positioned left="48" top="1058" width="1784"><Container alignment="CENTER_RIGHT"><Text fontSize="11" color="#6C7C99FF" fontFamily="Noto Sans Mono CJK SC" fontStyle="NORMAL">schematic · not a data plot</Text></Container></Positioned>
